{"objective":"EKA v2: (1) turn EKA into a solo-author knowledge system by removing work-ownership - the `mbr` token, the `assigned-to` relationship, `eka assign/reassign/unassign`, the MCP workspace assignment tools and the ownership prose in the skill pack - via deprecate-in-place, NOT hard-delete; (2) introduce a new product `eka-steward`, a thin deterministic consumer that drives a project from discussion to execution by orchestrating existing AI coding-agent CLIs over the Agent Client Protocol (ACP).\n\nAttribution/provenance (`author`/`by`/`by-kind`, including `worker`) is retained: it is provenance, not assignment. Method for removal is deprecate-in-place: authoring + surface are removed, but `mbr`/`assigned-to` stay recognized on read for one major so frozen `exchange/snapshots`, `shr` sharing objects and this repo's self-hosting `AGENTS.md` keep loading. A one-shot `eka migrate v2` scans the live store, unlinks `assigned-to` edges and retires `mbr` lines (forward-only).\n\neka-steward (`eka steward`, repo/binary eka-steward) is a separate repo at version 2.0.0, part of the EKA v2 program but on its OWN tag/release train. Boundary: import `eka-core` as a Go library (pinned v2); CLI subprocess only as an escape hatch; MCP rejected. Steward is the SOLE EKA store writer; workers never write EKA.","scope":"EKA line (standard, eka-core, eka-cli, eka-mcp) plus the new eka-steward repo.\n\nM6 solo-author: deprecate-in-place removal of work-ownership. Remove the `mbr` token authoring and the `assigned-to` relationship authoring; remove `eka assign/reassign/unassign`; remove the MCP workspace assignment tools; remove ownership prose in the skill pack; remove the skill-pack role mapping (RoleVocabulary, ModeDelegate, mappings/*.toml, DELEGATION.txt). Keep `mbr`/`assigned-to` recognized on read for one major. Ship a one-shot `eka migrate v2` (scan live store, unlink `assigned-to` edges, retire `mbr` lines, forward-only). Verify `/eka-discuss` and `/eka-execute` remain coherent via their existing `mode: solo` path. Keep attribution (`author`/`by`/`by-kind`, incl. `worker`).\n\nM7 steward: build eka-steward 2.0.0 as a thin deterministic consumer/orchestrator. Determinism rule: CODE decides which/when/if, LLM decides what (LLM only inside ACP worker sessions). ACP pinned to v1; driver config `{tool,cmd,args}`; Claude Code + Codex need adapters; the client MUST answer `session/request_permission` (default-deny policy engine, never hang). Worker sandbox is mandatory in v1 - reuse existing patterns (env allow-list `pluginEnv`, process-group spawn hardening, checksum trust per ADR-028) plus per-worker git-worktree isolation. Steward is the sole EKA writer.\n\nVersion ladder: standard 2.0, core v2, cli 2.0.0, mcp 2.0.0, steward 2.0.0. RSF `serializationVersion`/representation-ids/machine-schema UNCHANGED; mcp manifest contract stays `v1`.","outOfScope":"Hard deletion of `mbr`/`assigned-to` - deferred: deprecate-in-place for one major; `eka migrate v2` retires store lines but reader tolerance is retained.\n\nAny change to attribution/provenance (`author`/`by`/`by-kind`, incl. `worker`).\n\nAny new/alternative agent protocol or replacing ACP; making steward a knowledge author or a second EKA store writer; worker-side EKA writes.\n\nRSF `serializationVersion` / representation-id / machine-schema changes; the mcp manifest contract version (stays `v1`).\n\nThe EKA acronym collision with OSS `EKA-Community/eka` ('Executable Knowledge Architecture') - recorded separately (fnd:eka-acronym-collision); it does NOT block v2.\n\nMulti-tenant / cloud control-plane concerns."}